  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 127 PM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nTonight through Sunday...High pressure at the surface, centered over \nthe Great Lakes region, will continue to settle into the forecast \narea.  Through much of the forecast period, a drier airmass will \nlikewise settle over the area.  This'll result in less humid \nconditions with above normal high temperatures each afternoon and \npleasantly cool low temperatures each morning through much of the \nweek.\n\nRain chances look to begin increasing across the region over the \nweekend.  The National Hurricane Center is currently monitoring an \narea of disorganized showers and storms associated with a trough \nover the far southwest Gulf of America (i.e. Invest 92L).  Global \nmodels are in agreement that this area of convection will become \nbetter organized later this week, with NHC giving it an 80% chance \nof tropical cyclone development at the posting of this discussion.\n\nWe too are, and will continue to, monitor this area of convection \nfor development this week.  It's too early to determine what, if \nany, impacts this potential system will have on the gulf coast or \nour forecast area.  Residents and visitors to the region should \nmonitor the latest forecast for the most up-to-date information. \nRegardless of development, models do indicate deep tropical moisture \nlifting into the region over the weekend, again bringing good \nchances for rain to the CWA. /19/",
